we've had apples brand new Mac Pro in  our office for just over a month and  it's clear that it's a beast of a  machine in terms of the overall package  that you get great performance  completely silent operation genius  engineering in a very solid and premium  design it's so good that we've realized  that the Mac Pro is complete overkill  for our workflow and that's the $15,000  model nowhere near the fully specked out  $53,000 model so we got to thinking what  if we compared it to the two-month-old  16 inch MacBook Pro to see how much more  performance we're actually getting with  the Mac Pro over the MacBook which is  definitely no slouch there's also one  very good feature the new 16 inch  MacBook Pro gets that you literally  cannot get on the Mac Pro at least not  yet and we'll talk about it in just a  minute before we get into performance  numbers including various benchmarks  like blender video editing and Final Cut  Pro and photo editing and Lightroom  classic I want to first point out the  benefits of buying each of these Macs  let's start off with why somebody would  buy the 16 inch MacBook Pro the biggest  reason is portability unlike the Mac Pro  you can take the MacBook Pro anywhere  open it up and get some work done if the  battery starts running low plug it in  and you're good to go  and just like the Mac Pro you get some  Thunderbolt 3 ports which can be used to  plug in some of the fastest accessories  and drives available as well as external  monitors like the new Pro display XDR  but even without that you're getting a  really great 16 inch display that  features 500 nits of brightness and  excellent color accuracy for photo and  video editing work not only that but the  bezels have finally been slimmed down to  look a lot better than before you also  get a very solid design with the best  trackpad you can get on any laptop as  well as the new magic keyboard that puts  the old butterfly keyboard to shame add  new and improved speakers and  microphones and you get a really solid  portable computer the 16 inch MacBook  Pro is great for basically anyone from a  basic user with some extra cash to spare  to a huge range of entrepreneurs and  small business owners who need this  MacBook Pro to do their work and make  some  now the MacPro on the other hand is a  different beast it's more of a  commercial grade computer that  definitely doesn't give you the best  bang for your buck but instead gives you  top-notch reliability for computing  tasks and performance that you simply  can't get on any other Mac while you do  pay at least $6,000 for the base model  everything from the Ram graphics storage  and even the processor is upgradable in  fact we saved over 2000 dollars by  upgrading the RAM ourselves instead of  buying it from Apple and that's just 192  gigs  instead of the maximum 1.5 terabytes of  RAM if you're interested in upgrading it  yourself check out our do-it-yourself  RAM upgrade guide at the end of this  video the ability to upgrade these  components makes it a much better deal  in fact there are still people using  2010 Mac Pro's because they've been able  to upgrade it over the years and that's  why people are willing to pay so much  more for the Mac Pro you also get the  flexibility of choosing your own display  based on the type of work you need it  for like high-end photo or video editing  the Mac Pro is very useful for  successful small business owners who  want the extra tax write-off in great  performance as well as larger businesses  and Studios who spend more money on  labor than computer hardware so with  that let's get into performance here are  the specs and prices of both of these  Macs so we can see that the Mac Pro gets  an advantage in basically every area  except for the ones Haribhai SSD  starting off with the processor  performance using Geekbench 5 the Mac  Pro's processor is about 76% faster in  terms of raw multi-core performance  which is a pretty decent jump in  performance seeing as it's only got four  more quarts  now running cinnamon chart 20 which is  more realistic test the Mac Pro is  scoring around 66% faster than the  MacBook Pro which can turbo boost up to  a higher 5.0 gigahertz instead of 4.4 on  the Mac Pro on the other hand the Mac  Pro was completely silent during this  test  unlike the MacBook which started  spinning up its fans and as expected the  Mac Pro is the clear winner by far now  moving on to graphics performance with  Geekbench 5s metal test the Mac Pro  totally obliterates the MacBook Pro with  close to three-and-a-half  time's the performance this is the Mac  Pros biggest advantage because you just  can't get this much graphics power on  any other Mac now before we test photo  editing in Lightroom classic let's get  into video editing with Final Cut Pro  the Mac Pro finished the graphics heavy  Bruce X test in half the time six  seconds versus 12 seconds which honestly  still isn't bad before one of the recent  Final Cut Pro updates the sixteen inch  MacBook Pro used to take a full 20  seconds to finish this test in the  15-inch MacBook Pro with the best Vega  20 graphics took 32 seconds so the  recent updates are greatly benefiting  the 16 inch MacBook now testing for case  stabilization the Mac Pro was almost  twice as fast but seven seconds isn't  too bad for a 20 second clip on the  MacBook Pro either moving on to  exporting a five-minute h.264 4k clip  which is the format that most youtubers  use we start to see some different  results  the MacBook Pro only takes around 50  seconds longer than the Mac Pro which is  surprising compared to the rest of the  results we were getting this is because  the MacBook Pros processor has  integrated graphics so it can take  advantage of Intel's quick sync  technology which greatly speeds up  export times for h.264 video the Mac Pro  is relying purely on RAW processing  performance so it's still really  impressive now running the same test but  using an 8-bit h.265 clip or HEV see the  MacBook Pro actually finishes the export  1 second quicker than the Mac Pro this  is definitely a shocker and there's one  very important reason for it the 16 inch  MacBook Pro features AMD's brand new  Navi based seven nanometer graphics  technology which includes new HEV C  Hardware encoding that you can't get on  the mac pro at least not yet  the Mac Pro is soon getting a w50 700x  graphics option which does have the new  Navi technology and we're going to get  our hands on that very soon so subscribe  right now if you don't want to miss out  on that but for now the MacBook Pro  finishes this test one second faster  for that reason now moving on to a  10-bit heb c export the mac pro finishes  over twice as fast as the macbook pro  and that's because even though the Navi  graphics does support 10 bit  a TVC encoding it's actually not enabled  yet we're still waiting on Apple to  update Mac OS to support it and then the  MacBook Pro will actually be quite a bit  faster than the Mac Pro for this test  now onto the last three tests which are  much heavier tasks like exporting a  five-minute 60fps c200 bra clip the  macro Pro takes over twice as long and  interestingly the Mac Pro won't get any  faster because the current Vega encoders  run at a maximum of 60 fps so it  literally can't get faster than 5  minutes even if you get the better  graphics options however the MacBook Pro  is able to play it back at 56 fps so  it's almost perfectly smooth which isn't  too bad of an editing experience if  you're willing to wait longer for the  export now for a 4.5 K red raw export  test the MacBook Pro yet again took over  twice as long to export and finally our  ek red raw to UHD export test actually  didn't take that much longer on the  MacBook Pro but of course it is over two  minutes faster on the Mac Pro and some  people need it to be as fast as possible  to save as much time as possible so with  video editing out of the way let's  finish off with exporting 500 raw photos  with effects in Lightroom classic in  this test the MacBook Pro took around  two and a half times longer which can be  a huge deal for those who edit a ton of  photos all the time now all of these  tests focused on exporting times but in  reality the editing smoothness matters  more and the MacBook Pro can handle most  of these tasks very well because of the  new Final Cut Pro updates and Navi  graphics so if you're ok with waiting  longer for exports the MacBook Pro is  definitely the better choice because  it's obviously much more affordable and  it's also portable unlike the Mac Pro  now if you absolutely need to save the  most amount of time and you're ok with  paying more for it there's no other  option than the Mac Pro overall we're  really impressed with this year's 16  inch MacBook Pro and we'd say that  almost everyone should just buy the  MacBook Pro and most people will  actually be completely fine with the  base $2,400 16 inch model which is  actually 400 dollars off on Amazon right  now and if you're still wondering why  the heck anyone who would pay 15  thousand dollars for a MacPro check out  our review video right over there and  click the circle about to subscribe for  more videos like this one thanks for  watching and we'll see you in the next  video  [Music]

